      <figcaption>You handle unfamiliar chemicals under a fume hood. hood is
      the same idea for unfamiliar software.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>hood is a supply-chain firewall. It sits between your fetching tools
    and the network, and it looks at what comes back before you get to run
    it. Public package, private package, a tarball behind
    <code>curl</code> — it is all the same to hood: unfamiliar bytes,
    inspected on your machine before they are handed to the tool that asked
    for them.</p>

    <p>Nothing about your workflow changes. You still type <code>npm ci</code>
    and <code>pip install</code>. Nothing leaves the building. This is an
    experiment, and it is honest about that.</p>

    <p>The facts, briefly:</p>
    <ul class="hood-facts">
      <li><strong>What it checks.</strong> Every artifact a fetching command
      pulls in. Known-good and known-bad hashes and PURLs are decided at
      once; anything unknown is decomposed by <a href="/cleave/">cleave</a>
      and classified by <a href="/scan/">Atomdrift Scan</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Where it runs.</strong> On your machine. No cloud scanner,
      no API key, no proprietary package shipped to a third party. No
      telemetry.</li>
      <li><strong>How it intercepts.</strong> Locally, and only for the
      command that asked. The host trust store is never touched, and nothing
      is left behind for other processes.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, it stops.</strong> A fetch fails closed if
      scanning cannot start. Local work — <code>go test</code>,
      <code>cargo build</code>, <code>npm test</code> — is left alone.</li>
      <li><strong>Why it can see new attacks.</strong> It judges what an
      artifact <em>does</em>, not what it is called. That is what lets it
      flag a previously unseen 0-day supply-chain attack rather than waiting
      for a feed to name it.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="admonition warning">
      <p class="admonition-title">This is an experiment</p>
      <p>hood is an early build, released quietly to see how it behaves in
      the field. Verdicts, flags, and output will change without notice.
      There is no stable release. Try it, break it, send back what you find —
      but do not put it anywhere you cannot take it out again. It may eat
      your cat.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <p>hood installs user-level shims for the fetching tools already on your
    <code>PATH</code>. No system proxy, no root CA, no package-manager
    reconfiguration. Anything not listed still runs under
    <code>hood exec -- &lt;command&gt;</code>. The columns compare hood with
    three related tools; only hood classifies the contents of a private
    artifact locally, rather than deciding from a feed or from metadata.</p>

